# Ambient type packages stay visible to tsc. Types are a compile-time concern; hiding them
# buys nothing but tsconfig archaeology. This is the ONLY concession to strict linking --
# no shamefully-hoist, no node-linker=hoisted. Strict linking is the point of the migration:
# it is what surfaces dependencies a package imports but never declares.

# NOTE: there is deliberately no `prod=false` here, even though the retired .yarnrc carried
# its yarn-1 equivalent. That hazard does NOT exist on pnpm -- measured, not assumed.
# See v5.2/pnpm-migration/prod-false-experiment.md (7 cells, positive control included):
# on pnpm 11.24.0 ONLY an explicit `--prod` CLI flag drops devDependencies. NODE_ENV=production
# and NPM_CONFIG_PRODUCTION=true are both ignored, and `prod=false` does not override the CLI
# flag anyway -- so the setting would have guarded nothing. Do not re-add it without new
# evidence; carrying it over would have been folklore, not a mitigation.

# agent-kit's `sync` discovers skills by scanning the ROOT node_modules for packages
# that ship a skills/ directory. Under yarn's hoisted tree every transitive @mongez
# and every workspace @warlock.js package was visible there, so it found them all.
# pnpm's strict layout exposes only what the ROOT itself declares — 7 of 23 @mongez
# packages and none of the workspace ones — so a sync silently rebuilt the catalogue
# from a fraction of its sources and deleted the rest.
#
# This hoists them for TOOLING VISIBILITY ONLY. It does not change what any package
# can resolve at runtime, so it does not weaken the strict-linking property this
# migration exists to get: every package still has to declare what it imports.
